1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,040
>> Around 240 BC, a scholar in Egypt worked out the size of the whole Earth.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,310
>> Without ever going round it?

3
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:13,550
>> He didn't need to. He used a shadow, a distance and one idea from geometry.

4
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:18,070
>> And his answer was about 250,000 stadia.

5
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:23,140
>> Which sounds precise. But even today, we can't say exactly how close he came.

6
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:29,660
>> So this is the story of Eratosthenes, and of a number we still argue about.

7
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:42,950
Eratosthenes was born around 276 BC in Cyrene, a Greek city in what's now Libya.

8
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:49,370
>> He studied in Athens for years. Then the king of Egypt, Ptolemy the Third, called him to Alexandria.

9
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:55,140
>> To run the Library of Alexandria, one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.

10
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:56,700
>> Quite a job offer.

11
00:00:56,780 --> 00:01:00,470
And the post came with another duty: tutor to the future king.

12
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:05,620
>> He wrote about mathematics, geography, poetry, history and the stars.

13
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:10,600
>> His admirers called him Pentathlos, like an athlete who competes in five events.

14
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:14,600
>> And his critics called him Beta: second best at everything.

15
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:18,360
>> Second best at everything is still quite something.

16
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:22,380
>> One thing he didn't have to prove was that the Earth is round.

17
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:23,960
>> They already knew that?

18
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:30,610
>> For a century or so. Aristotle had pointed to the Earth's round shadow on the Moon during an eclipse.

19
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:34,990
>> And he noted that some stars seen in Egypt can't be seen from further north.

20
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:39,040
>> Which only works if the ground curves between the two places.

21
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:43,700
>> Aristotle even reported a size for the Earth: 400,000 stadia.

22
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:48,620
>> Eratosthenes found a way to actually measure it.

23
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:57,240
>> Far up the Nile, on Egypt's southern frontier, lay the town of Syene. Today it's Aswan.

24
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:01,310
>> And Syene was known for something that happened there once a year.

25
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:07,930
>> At noon on the summer solstice, the longest day of the year, upright sticks there cast no shadow.

26
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:09,350
>> None at all?

27
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:15,930
>> None. Later writers also tell of a well there, where the noon sunlight reached all the way down to the water.

28
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:18,800
>> Because the Sun was standing straight overhead.

29
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:25,070
>> Syene was taken to lie on the Tropic of Cancer, the furthest north the Sun can ever be overhead.

30
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:32,810
>> But at Alexandria, on the coast, about 5,000 stadia further north, sticks still cast shadows at that noon.

31
00:02:32,890 --> 00:02:34,960
>> And that difference is the whole clue.

32
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:41,000
If the Earth were flat, and the Sun far away, sticks in both places would cast the same shadow.

33
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:44,720
>> They don't. So the ground between them has to curve.

34
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:49,130
>> And how much it curves over that distance tells you the size of the whole Earth.

35
00:02:49,210 --> 00:02:56,130
>> So he needs two numbers: how different the shadows are, and how far apart the cities are.

36
00:02:58,730 --> 00:03:01,640
So how big was the shadow at Alexandria?

37
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:06,340
>> According to Cleomedes, it was read on a scaphe: a sundial shaped like a bowl.

38
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:09,260
>> A bowl, not just a stick in the ground?

39
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:15,490
>> A bowl. A pointer stands at the bottom, and its tip sits right at the centre of the bowl's curve.

40
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:19,710
>> So at noon, the tip's shadow falls on the inside of the bowl.

41
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:25,470
>> The arc from the foot of the pointer to the end of the shadow measures how far the Sun is from overhead.

42
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:30,540
>> That's neat. The bowl turns the shadow straight into a piece of a circle.

43
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:35,230
And at Alexandria, that arc was one fiftieth of a full circle.

44
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:38,560
>> In degrees, that's 7.2°.

45
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:44,550
>> That's small. A stick would cast a noon shadow only about one eighth as long as itself.

46
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:50,120
>> And at Syene, at the same noon, the shadow was nothing at all.

47
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:58,960
Now for the key idea. The Sun is so far away that its rays arrive almost parallel.

48
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:04,180
>> Let's draw the Earth, with Syene at the top and Alexandria a little way round.

49
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:10,980
>> At Syene, a ray comes straight down the stick. Extend that stick downwards, and it reaches the Earth's centre.

50
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:15,950
>> At Alexandria, extend the stick too. It also points to the centre.

51
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:20,060
>> And there, the ray meets the stick at 7.2°.

52
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:28,060
>> Now the Alexandria stick line crosses two parallel lines: the ray at Alexandria, and the ray down the Syene stick.

53
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:29,770
>> That's the Z shape.

54
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:34,800
When a line crosses two parallel lines, the alternate angles are equal.

55
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:41,120
>> So the angle at the centre of the Earth, between the two cities, is also 7.2°.

56
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:45,290
>> So the shadow in Alexandria is really measuring the curve of the Earth.

57
00:04:45,370 --> 00:04:51,060
Syene and Alexandria are one fiftieth of the way round the Earth.

58
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:54,800
>> But is the Sun really far enough away for that?

59
00:04:54,880 --> 00:05:01,730
>> Easily. Between those two cities, the rays differ by less than a thousandth of a degree.

60
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:07,620
>> Now he needed the distance between the two cities.

61
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:10,730
>> Cleomedes gives it as 5,000 stadia.

62
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:12,650
>> And how was that measured?

63
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:15,150
>> Honestly, we don't really know.

64
00:05:15,230 --> 00:05:21,600
>> Egypt did have bematists: professional pacers who measured land for farming and for taxes.

65
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,300
>> And some historians think his distance came from their surveys.

66
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:31,070
>> A Latin writer, some 650 years later, says royal surveyors measured it.

67
00:05:31,150 --> 00:05:36,380
>> Six hundred and fifty years later. That's a long way from a first-hand account.

68
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:43,760
Either way: if 5,000 stadia is one fiftieth of the way round, the whole way round is fifty times as far.

69
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:49,660
>> Fifty times 5,000: 250,000 stadia.

70
00:05:50,710 --> 00:05:55,560
>> A shadow, a distance, and the size of the whole Earth.

71
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:03,030
>> That's the story as it's usually told. But where does it actually come from?

72
00:06:03,110 --> 00:06:08,500
>> Eratosthenes wrote it up in a book, On the Measure of the Earth. And the book is lost.

73
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:11,320
>> So we're relying on someone else's account.

74
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:15,160
>> Mostly on Cleomedes, who wrote a basic astronomy textbook.

75
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:22,080
>> And nobody knows when he wrote it: somewhere between the first century BC and about AD 400.

76
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:24,840
>> That's a window of more than four centuries.

77
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:28,510
And Cleomedes says himself that his version is simplified.

78
00:06:28,590 --> 00:06:35,750
>> His account talks about sundials, not a well. The well comes from other writers, like the geographer Strabo.

79
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:39,740
>> So the most famous detail isn't even in our main source.

80
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:41,760
>> And there's a second number.

81
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:47,980
>> The Roman writer Pliny the Elder gives Eratosthenes' result as 252,000 stadia.

82
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:51,900
>> Why the extra 2,000? Historians disagree.

83
00:06:51,980 --> 00:07:01,260
>> 252,000 divides evenly by every whole number from one to ten. 250,000 doesn't.

84
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:07,450
>> And split into 360 degrees, it gives a neat 700 stadia for each one.

85
00:07:07,530 --> 00:07:11,860
>> So some think he adjusted his figure, to make calculations easier.

86
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:18,650
>> Others think he defined a stadion of his own, from the size of the Earth, as Pliny's wording suggests.

87
00:07:18,730 --> 00:07:22,810
>> Which would make the unit itself part of the puzzle.

88
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:30,380
So how good was his answer? To check, we need his stadia in kilometres.

89
00:07:30,460 --> 00:07:36,300
>> A stadion was 600 feet. But a foot wasn't the same everywhere in the Greek world.

90
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:41,820
>> Historians' estimates for the stadion run from about 150 to 210 metres.

91
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:44,140
>> That's a spread of 40%.

92
00:07:44,220 --> 00:07:50,810
And the real distance round the Earth, through the poles, is about 40,008 kilometres.

93
00:07:50,890 --> 00:07:55,130
>> So here's what that range of stadia does to his 250,000.

94
00:07:55,210 --> 00:08:00,820
>> With a stadion of about 176 metres, he's about 10% too big.

95
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:05,100
>> With about 185 metres, he's about 15% too big.

96
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:16,040
>> But take Pliny's 252,000 and a short stadion, 155 to 160 metres, and he's within 2.4%.

97
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:18,680
>> So is the short stadion the right one?

98
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:26,750
>> One study found about 157.7 metres from ancient distances, but critics say it's full of errors.

99
00:08:26,830 --> 00:08:31,780
>> So the famous claim that he was within 2% depends on choosing a short stadion.

100
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:34,630
>> That's a lot to hang on one unit.

101
00:08:34,710 --> 00:08:42,380
>> But the method itself is sound: measure the angle and the distance accurately, and it gives Earth's true size.

102
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:50,070
>> Cleomedes' version also rests on a few simplifications. Let's check them.

103
00:08:50,150 --> 00:08:54,480
>> First, parallel rays. That one holds very well, as we've seen.

104
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:58,650
>> Second, that Syene sits exactly on the Tropic of Cancer.

105
00:08:58,730 --> 00:09:05,110
>> In fact it's a little north of it, by less than a degree. Even there, the noon Sun wasn't quite overhead.

106
00:09:05,190 --> 00:09:07,800
>> So "no shadow" was only nearly true.

107
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:15,720
And the tropic drifts, too. In Eratosthenes' time it lay about 0.3 degrees further north than today.

108
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:20,730
>> Third, that Syene lies due south of Alexandria, on the same meridian.

109
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:24,800
>> In fact it's about 3 degrees of longitude further east.

110
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:30,930
>> And the Earth isn't a perfect sphere: it's slightly flattened, by about 0.3%.

111
00:09:31,010 --> 00:09:37,770
>> Still, the angle came out well. The two cities' latitudes really differ by about 7.1°.

112
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:40,950
>> Against his 7.2. That's close.

113
00:09:41,030 --> 00:09:46,320
What we can't check is the distance, because we don't know his stadion.

114
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:52,260
>> Measuring the Earth was only part of what he did.

115
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:57,510
>> In his Geographica, he mapped the known world on a grid of parallels and meridians.

116
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:03,550
>> It named more than 400 cities with their locations, and it gave us the word geography.

117
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:09,850
>> He also measured the tilt of the Earth's axis, and worked out dates in history back to the fall of Troy.

118
00:10:09,930 --> 00:10:14,800
>> And his name lives on in mathematics, in the sieve of Eratosthenes.

119
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:17,000
>> The way of finding prime numbers?

120
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:23,470
>> Write out the numbers. Cross out the multiples of 2, then of 3, then of 5, then of 7.

121
00:10:23,550 --> 00:10:26,990
>> And what's left are the prime numbers.

122
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:34,880
>> Though our earliest record of the sieve was written centuries later, and he may have named it rather than invented it.

123
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:41,530
>> Later writers used smaller figures for the Earth. Ptolemy's Geography used just 180,000 stadia.

124
00:10:41,610 --> 00:10:50,380
>> And some seventeen centuries after Eratosthenes, Columbus read his work, but chose to believe in an Earth about a quarter smaller.

125
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:55,280
>> And a smaller Earth made the voyage west to Asia look shorter.

126
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:58,230
>> So let's put the whole idea in one picture.

127
00:10:58,310 --> 00:11:03,460
>> No shadow at Syene. A shadow of one fiftieth of a circle at Alexandria.

128
00:11:03,540 --> 00:11:07,410
>> Parallel rays carry that angle to the centre of the Earth.

129
00:11:07,490 --> 00:11:12,340
>> Fifty times 5,000 stadia: 250,000 stadia round.

130
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:17,060
>> How close was he? That depends on a unit we still can't pin down.

131
00:11:17,140 --> 00:11:22,080
>> But the idea was right, and it measured the whole Earth from two cities in Egypt.

132
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:25,550
>> With a stick, a bowl and a good argument.

133
00:11:25,630 --> 00:11:29,650
>> Not bad for a man his critics called Beta.
